 I did  report it but nothing was done in fact I  was it was brushed under the carpet it  was made to sort of made out that it was  a sort of minor minor incident and what  did I expect and what did I want them to  do about it because yeah because  presumably there is well was there a  procedure in place there was no HR  process there were no HR structure I  told my manager and in fact I also had  some issues with him he was he used to  shout at me quite a lot our offices were  next door to each other and he would  shout rather than pick the phone up and  ask me to go in or come in and see me he  would just shout my name until I went in  one occasion I went in and he said oh oh  I just want to tell you it's a really  nice dress that you've got on this it's  the person that you had to report an  assault to yeah he was the only person I  could report it to doesn't mean anyone  else and there was an HR department but  that was it was made clear that was for  members and members staff it wasn't for  House staff he was you were sort of not  there wasn't any way for you to go  basically so so I said that to him and  that was his response and I felt very  vulnerable  I felt very exposed but I also felt like  I just needed to toughen up a bit and  this sort of word resilience has been  banded around quite a lot and I think  you were made to feel that you needed to  be resilient if you need if he wanted to  get on in that organisation and  obviously that is not the definition of  resilience is putting up with that kind  of behavior but at any point feel you  should go to the police did you ever  consider that oh well and this was as I  say in the 90s  after I contacted Andrea led some to  tell her my story I got a response to  say you should go to the police so I did  so I went to the police quite recently  because at the time you wanted to keep  your job I wanted to keep my job and I  because it would play down oh this is  how everyone everyone has this and one  comment that I've read in the paper you  haven't earned your stripes until you've  been assaulted but it's Italy and that  that's exactly how it felt it felt that  this was just part and parcel of that  culture of this very sort of insular  culture and that's what this report that  came out last week demonstrated that  this is widespread is endemic it seems  to be in the fabric of Westminster  absolutely when you were going through  your ordeal did you speak to your  colleagues about any experiences they  might had have did you see other things  going on around you to make you feel  that you weren't the only one oh yes  absolutely I wasn't the only one I mean  I told people about the incident with  that MP because he then went on to stalk  me for years  and nothing was done about it and like  day to day was awful I used to hide for  if I thought because he worked in the  same building as me so I used to hide on  the same floor actually so if I saw him  I would I would you know make a beeline  for the loo or I would hide or he would  find me and and I mean he you know he'd  make a beeline for me in in staff  cafeteria and he would come and sit with  me yeah yeah they see still in  Westminster now no but he yeah he would  sort of make a beeline for me and my  friends knew oh god here he comes  they would know to to help and they  would sort of we would sort of share our  experiences of members behaving badly  but it was just you were just made to  feel as if you just had to get on with  it you just had to suck it up because at  the end of the day it's not really that  big a deal there are more important  things that's that's the message there  are more important things we were  written about so it's always the price  that women and some men  have to pay for a career in Westminster  lately yeah I mean some of what you're  describing to me we're not talking about  one-off incident no instance we're  talking about your daily work your life  yeah being affected yes my bullying yes  harassment yes and it affects me now  yeah how am I you know and I struggle  I've been diagnosed with PTSD  I have sleepless nights yeah I mean you  know this is really scary to come to  come and do this but it's so important  because I don't think people appreciate  how damaging it is to people and that's  that's the message I wanted to kind of  get across is that this is affecting  everyday people that it's affecting  their lives it's affecting their careers  you know I I felt there was no choice  but for me to leave the house because I  couldn't cope with it any longer and  that must be so disappointing for you in  terms of I would imagine going into  Westminster you imagined a long lasting  worthwhile career it's a career for life  and you you know I really value the  importance that Parliament plays I loved  working for an institution that was  doing such good work it was really  important to me I was passionate about  my my job was passionate about doing  something of value and and yeah so it  did feel like it was going to be for  life we're doing something positive now  in terms of you using your experience to  speak up with the Solidarity of others  who have been through the same thing to  help women in politics to help women in  Westminster and men who are vulnerable  as well we've had stories of men who  have been through similar experiences  what do you want to come from this  report in this discussion that's  happening today and going forward and I  want I want historic cases to be looked  at because I not alone there are lots of  other cases that weren't even looked at  there are lots of people that have  grievances I'm sure that haven't you  have a chance to talk about them so it's  really important for me that historic  cases are looked at I want an apology  really I want I want someone to take  responsibility for what's happened and I  want management to accept that there  there is a problem

